A||||5||cifc||yet another question about GA brakes||||5||
Z||000000||cifc||10-29-2000||06:08 PM||||Earlier this year I put the Grand am brakes front and rear on my 87.  I had no problems at all with that.  Now, I'm trying to put the GA brakes on the front of my 85 and keep the Fiero brakes on the back in order to have an e-brake.  I bought all new calipers and ss brake lines.  I also put in a master cylinder from an 84 full size blazer, wich is what I'm using on the 87. The problem is that I can't get the fronts to work or bleed properly.  I'm thinking that the rear calipers are filling up with fluid, and not letting the pedal travel far enough to fill up the fronts.  When I realease the bleeder in the rear the pedal travels all the way to the floor.  Does this make sense to anyone?  I thin kI'll need to put the GA brakes on the back until I can get one of the larger brake kits.  ANy other ideas?<BR>--Jason ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-29-2000||07:23 PM||||It sounds like your proportioning valve has stuck. Replace it and see if that cures your problems.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-29-2000||08:36 PM||||If you didn't bench bleed your M/C first, it can be a pain to bleed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-29-2000||09:42 PM||||The problem likely <I>is not</I> the rear calipers.<P>It's likely not the prop valve either. the valve is on the back brake circuit.<P>You either have air trapped some place or the front lines are blocked. You could also have a bad master cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Terrybogin||10-29-2000||10:15 PM||||When you figure it out, let us know what was wrong so when we add the GA brakes we dont have the same problems, please!||||reg||1
Z||000005||cifc||10-30-2000||11:27 AM||||This is really starting to bother me.  I don't think there can possibly be any more air in there.  I've bled the brakes so much that I've gone through two 32 oz bottles of brake fluid.  I'm getting clean fluid at all the calipers.  I suppose the front lines could be partially blocked.  I think I'll try replacing the master cylinder.  I guess I should've mentioned that the car has only been driven once in the past 5 or six years.  That was one scary drive.  Thanks for the advice.<BR>--Jason||||reg||1
